        "section": [
          {
            "text": {
              "status": "generated",
              "div": "<div xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\">Ectopic tubal pregnancy, surgically treated</div>"
            },
            "code": {
              "coding": [
                {
                  "code": "67781-5",
                  "system": "http://loinc.org",
                  "display": "Summarization of encounter note"
                }
              ]
            },
            "title": "Synthesis"
          },
          {
            "text": {
              "status": "generated",
              "div": "<div xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\">Dear colleague, I am writing to inform you about the hospital course of your 40-year-old female patient, admitted on 25/04/2017 with suspected tubal pregnancy. She presented with acute lower abdominal pain and vaginal spotting. On admission, physical examination and transvaginal ultrasonography confirmed a right-sided tubal ectopic pregnancy without signs of rupture. Serum beta-hCG levels were significantly elevated. Given the clinical findings and patient's hemodynamic stability, a laparoscopic salpingectomy was performed on 26/04/2017 without intraoperative complications. Histopathology confirmed ectopic trophoblastic tissue within the fallopian tube. Postoperatively, the patient recovered well and was discharged on 27/04/2017 in stable condition. She has been counseled regarding future fertility and the risk of recurrent ectopic pregnancy. Further follow-up including serial beta-hCG measurements is recommended. Please do not hesitate to contact me for any further information.</div>"
            },
            "code": {
